{"packet_id":"D040-01","doc_id":"D040","jurisdiction":"Los Angeles, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Los Angeles Just Cause Ordinance (JCO)","requirement":"A landlord may end a covered tenancy only for a just cause including non-payment of rent, nuisance, lease violation, owner move-in, demolition or government order. No-fault evictions require relocation assistance; since March 27, 2023, unpaid rent at or below Fair Market Rent is not cause.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Covers most City of Los Angeles residential properties not regulated by the Rent Stabilization Ordinance, including buildings newer than October 1, 1978 and single-family dwellings; the tenant must have lived in the unit six months or had an expired lease.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","text":"Properties not regulated by the City's Rent Stabilization Ordinance"},{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"HACLA or the government","unit_limit":null}],"per_tenancy":"The tenant must have lived in the same unit for at least six months or their original lease must have expired, whichever comes first.","coverage_quotes":["The JCO covers most residential properties in the City of Los Angeles that are not regulated by the City’s Rent Stabilization Ordinance (RSO).","There are other specific exceptions to the JCO including, but not limited to, transient hotels, licensed care facilities, fraternity or sorority houses, owner’s roommate, cooperatives under certain circumstances, some non-profit facilities for the homeless or short-term treatment centers for substance abuse, and some properties owned by HACLA or the government.","In order to apply to a tenancy, it requires that the tenant either has lived in the same unit for at least six months or that their original lease expired, whichever comes first."]},"exemptions":"Transient hotels; licensed care facilities; fraternity or sorority houses; owner's roommate; cooperatives under certain circumstances; some non-profit facilities for the homeless or short-term treatment centers for substance abuse; properties owned by HACLA or the government; units regulated by the Rent Stabilization Ordinance.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"Los Angeles Just Cause Ordinance (L.A.M.C. §165.00 et seq.)","quoted_span":"It prohibits terminations of tenancies without just cause and requires relocation assistance for no-fault evictions.","interaction":"The JCO does not regulate rent increases; state law AB 1482 may regulate rents in buildings older than 15 years.","relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D040-01","doc_id":"D040","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"California Tenant Protection Act (AB 1482) annual rent cap","requirement":"For covered units, annual rent increases are limited to 5% plus the regional cost-of-living change, or 10%, whichever is lower. The maximum allowable increase from August 1, 2024 to July 31, 2025 is 8.9%.","key_value":"5% + CPI, max 10%; 8.9% for August 1, 2024 to July 31, 2025","coverage_conditions":"Buildings older than 15 years, except units built within the last 15 years, deed-restricted affordable units, certain dormitories, owner-occupied two-unit properties, qualifying single-family homes and condominiums, and units already under the City's RSO.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"built_within_years","role":"exempt","years":15,"basis":"construction"},{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied","unit_limit":2},{"type":"other","text":"Units already subject to the City's Rent Stabilization Ordinance"},{"type":"other","text":"Units restricted by a deed or recorded document limiting affordability to low- or moderate-income households"}],"per_tenancy":"Single-family homes and condominiums are exempt from state law only if not owned by a real estate trust, corporation or LLC with a corporate member and the landlord notified the tenant in writing that the tenancy is not subject to just cause or rent limits.","coverage_quotes":["-Units that were constructed within the last 15 years (this applies on a rolling basis – i.e.. a unit constructed on January 1, 2006 is not covered as of January, 1 2020, but is covered on and after January 1, 2021).","-A two-unit property, provided the second unit was occupied by an owner of the property for the entire period of the tenancy.","-Units that are already subject to the City’s RSO."]},"exemptions":"Units constructed within the last 15 years; units restricted by deed or recorded document to low- or moderate-income households; certain dormitories; two-unit properties where an owner occupied the second unit for the tenancy; single-family homes and condominiums meeting the ownership and notice conditions; units already subject to the City's RSO.","penalty":null,"effective_date":"2024-08-01","valid_through":"2025-07-31","citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1947.12","quoted_span":"Effective August 1, 2024 to July 31, 2025, the maximum allowable increase is 8.9%.","interaction":"State law; the City's JCO does not regulate rent increases.","relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
